proof.text:
Mark $E$ by its $p$-Cartesian edges, writing $E^{\mathrm{Cart}}$ for this marked object. In the absolute marked model choose functorially a trivial cofibration $q_p:E^{\mathrm{Cart}}	o L_p^{
atural}$ with fibrant target. Thus $L_p$ is a small quasicategory, $q_p$ sends Cartesian edges to equivalences, and for every quasicategory $T$ the definition of marked weak equivalence gives
$$\operatorname{Map}^{\sharp}(L_p^{
atural},T^{
atural})\simeq\operatorname{Map}^{\sharp}(E^{\mathrm{Cart}},T^{
atural}).$$
Here $\operatorname{Map}^{ lat}(E^{\mathrm{Cart}},T^{
atural})$ is exactly the full quasicategory $H_p(T)=\operatorname{Fun}_{\mathrm{Cart}(p)}(E,T)$: an $n$-simplex is a map $E	imes\Delta^n	o T$ whose restriction at every vertex of $\Delta^n$ inverts the marked edges. By [lem-coef-marked-anodyne-lifting-calculus], which also fixes the notation $\operatorname{Map}^{ lat}$, $\operatorname{Map}^{ lat}(E^{\mathrm{Cart}},T^{
atural})$ is a quasicategory and $\operatorname{Map}^{\sharp}(E^{\mathrm{Cart}},T^{
atural})$ is exactly its core. The same node says that $T^{
atural}$ has the right lifting property against every marked-anodyne map; by the criterion of [lem-coef-relative-marked-anodyne-calculus] over $S=\Delta^0$, $T^{
atural}$ is therefore the natural marking of the Cartesian fibration $T	o\Delta^0$, so the Cartesian edges of $T	o\Delta^0$ are exactly the equivalences of $T$. Consequently the preceding equivalence is induced by actual restriction along $q_p$, and reads $\operatorname{Map}_{\mathrm{Cat}_\infty}(L_p,T)\simeq H_p(T)^{\simeq}$. Replacement and these tests can be formed in any larger universe; no size bound on $T$ beyond belonging to that universe is used.

We strengthen the core comparison of step 1 to the required full functor-category comparison. Fix $T$ and let $A$ be any quasicategory. The marked-product formula and the pointwise criterion for equivalences give natural identifications
$$\operatorname{Map}^{\sharp}(A^{
atural}	imes L_p^{
atural},T^{
atural})=\operatorname{Fun}(A,\operatorname{Fun}(L_p,T))^{\simeq},$$
$$\operatorname{Map}^{\sharp}(A^{
atural}	imes E^{\mathrm{Cart}},T^{
atural})=\operatorname{Fun}(A,H_p(T))^{\simeq}.$$
For the second identity the marking condition is equivalent to the vertexwise condition defining $H_p(T)$: for an equivalence $a	o a'$ in $A$ and a Cartesian $e:x	o y$ in $E$, the product arrow factors, with its product composition simplex, through $(a,x)	o(a,y)	o(a',y)$. The first factor is inverted by that vertexwise condition and the second by functoriality on equivalences in $A$. Conversely take the $A$-arrow to be an identity. The parameter sharp simplices on both sides encode pointwise equivalences of transformations, by the cited pointwise criterion, so these are identifications on all simplices. Products with any marked object preserve absolute marked weak equivalences. Hence $A^{
atural}	imes q_p$ is a weak equivalence, and testing against $T^{
atural}$ shows that restriction $j:\operatorname{Fun}(L_p,T)	o H_p(T)$ induces an equivalence on mapping spaces out of every $A$ in $\mathrm{Cat}_\infty$. Explicitly take $A=H_p(T)$ to obtain $g$ and $jg\simeq1$; take $A=\operatorname{Fun}(L_p,T)$ and injectivity on components to deduce $gj\simeq1$. Thus $j$ is an equivalence of quasicategories. Compose it with restriction along $k_p^{-1}$ to obtain the stated equivalence induced by $\ell_p$. All noninvertible transformations and their mapping spaces are retained in these whole functor categories; the argument does not infer this merely from an equivalence of cores.
